<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Shapes Around Us – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Shapes Around Us</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> That Is a Circle.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write numbers up to ten with guidance. Interactive: Write one-word answers to simple questions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write numbers and shape-related words with teacher guidance (e.g., counting the number of squares in a picture and writing the number and the shape's name below the picture). Can label pictures of shapes with simple words like "square" or "circle." Can write simple one-word answers to questions about shapes with support (e.g., writing the word "circle" in response to the question, "What shape is this?").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Shape Tracing and Naming" activity. Students use their fingers to trace large shapes (circle, square, triangle, rectangle) drawn on the whiteboard or on individual worksheets. As they trace, they say the shape's name aloud. Then, the teacher distributes a worksheet with dotted outlines of shape names (e.g., "circle," "square") and numbers (1-5). Students trace the letters and numbers, practicing fine motor skills for writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up flashcards of shapes and explicitly models how to trace the shape name and numbers (e.g., "circle", "1") on the board, enunciating each letter sound and number name clearly. The teacher then writes "That is a circle." and "I see two squares." demonstrating correct letter formation and spacing. Pronunciation of /s/ in "square" and /r/ in "rectangle" is reinforced by exaggerating the sounds while writing the words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a picture of a square and asks: "Is this a circle?" (Expected answer: "No.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to the word "triangle" on the board and asks: "What is this word?" (Expected answer: "Triangle.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Shapes Around Us")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, colorful "Shapes Poster" depicting various everyday objects that are examples of shapes (e.g., a round clock for circle, a square window for square, a rectangular door for rectangle, a slice of pizza for triangle). Each object has a simple sentence underneath it, such as "That is a circle." or "This is a square table." Numbers 1-10 are also displayed next to small groups of shapes.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identifying shapes: Recognize and name shapes like squares and circles during classroom activities. Describing shapes: Use simple phrases to talk about shapes (e.g., "This is a rectangle.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to a shape on the poster and asks: "What shape is this?" (Students respond chorally or individually).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to the written sentence "That is a circle." and asks: "What does it say?" (Students repeat the sentence).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to a group of three triangles and asks: "How many triangles do you see?" (Students count and say "Three."). The teacher then writes the number '3' and the word 'triangle' on the board.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice writing shape names (square, rectangle, circle, triangle, oval, diamond, star) and numbers (1-10) using target grammar (WH-questions, Present simple, Shape + noun). They will also reinforce phonemic awareness of initial sounds (e.g., /s/ in "square").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Shape Name & Number Tracing:</b> Students receive a worksheet with pictures of shapes (e.g., a circle, a square). Below each shape, there is a dotted outline of its name and a number (e.g., "_____circle_____" or "_____square_____" and "_____3_____"). Students trace the shape names and numbers multiple times. The teacher circulates, checking posture and pencil grip, and providing individual guidance on letter formation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: Label the Shapes and Count:</b> The teacher gives students a worksheet with various pictures of shapes, some in groups. For example, there might be a picture of "a small circle," "a big rectangle," or a group of "five stars." Below each picture, there is a blank line or a sentence frame like "This is a ______." or "I see ______ (number) ______ (shape)." Students write the correct shape name or count the shapes and write the number and name in the blanks. A word bank of shape names and numbers 1-10 is provided on the board for support.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction: Draw, Write, and Check:</b> Students work in pairs. Each student draws three different shapes in their notebook. For each shape, they write a simple sentence or label, such as "This is a triangle." or "Two squares." (using the grammar: Shape + noun, Present simple). Then, they exchange notebooks with their partner. The partner checks the spelling of the shape names and the clarity of the numbers. The teacher monitors, providing feedback and correcting common errors, emphasizing the /s/ sound in "square" and /r/ sound in "rectangle."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Shapes Around Us")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative "My Shapes Book" creation. Students will create a small, personalized "My Shapes Book" where they draw, count, and write about shapes they see around them.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students receive blank mini-books (folded paper).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">On each page, they draw a different shape or an object representing that shape (e.g., a door for rectangle, a sun for circle). They can use colors to describe them (e.g., "The sun is yellow.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Below each drawing, they *write* a simple sentence or label to describe it, focusing on the target structures. Examples: "This is a circle." "That is a big square." "I see three stars." "What shape is this? It is a rectangle." (Teacher provides sentence starters and a word bank on the board for support).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They also practice writing numbers by indicating how many of each shape they drew or saw.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students present their "My Shapes Book" to a small group or the class, pointing to their drawings and reading their written descriptions.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during "My Shapes Book" creation and presentation, combined with a quick exit ticket.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students complete their "My Shapes Book" and answer a short written exit ticket.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can write numbers 1-10 with guidance.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can write names of basic shapes (circle, square, triangle, rectangle, oval, star, diamond) with guidance/support.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can write a one-word answer to "What shape is it?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses simple phrases like "That is a circle." or "This is a big square." in writing.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Shows effort in letter formation and neatness.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Draw one shape you like. Write its name below the drawing.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Write one number from your "Shapes Book."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new shape word did you write today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to write the numbers?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this writing lesson? (e.g., tracing activities, use of visual aids, partner work for checking).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for students struggling with writing shape names or numbers? (e.g., more tracing practice, larger print, more direct modeling, focusing on fewer words/numbers).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align="justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The writing practice from today's lesson will be integrated into the 21st Century Skills Project in Lesson 5, where students will apply their knowledge of shapes and writing skills to create a collaborative poster or presentation about shapes they find in their school environment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>